{
  "name": "epanet_relief_near_set",
  "description": "The relief network pressurized just above the valve's set, as an EPANET GPV behind a CV pipe: verifies the start of the lift, where the flow is most sensitive to the pressure, against EPANET 2.2.",
  "source": "WNTR 1.5.0 EpanetSimulator (EPANET 2.2.0); see tests/reference/generate_epanet_cases.py. D-W headloss, GPM INP units, accuracy 1e-5, headerror 1e-9 ft and flowchange 1e-12 gpm (EPANET reads both in the INP units, input1.c:595-596; WNTR writes them unconverted); viscosity VISCOS = 1.1e-5 ft2/s = 1.02193344e-6 m2/s (the project's custom fluid has the same nu; its density, 1000 kg/m3, does not affect the comparison because friction depends only on nu and a surface-pressure head p/(rho g) is converted with the same rho and g on both sides); solver run in 'swamee_jain' mode. Known EPANET biases, all far inside the 0.5% criterion: EPANET's head-loss coefficients use g = 32.2 ft/s2 = 9.81456 m/s2 against our 9.80665 (0.08% in head loss), its minor-loss constant is 0.02517 against the exact 1/(2g(pi/4)^2) = 0.0251730 in US units (0.012%), its GPM flow-unit constant GPMperCFS = 448.831 differs from the exact ft3/(gal/60) by -3.8e-07 (relative, in flow), and its results are float32 (about 1e-7 relative). Relief valves: EPANET GPV between junctions at its ports' elevations, diameter of the inlet pipe, minor loss 0, whose HEADLOSS curve tabulates the valve's EGL law (element_laws.ReliefLaw: h_set + (dp_t(Q) - dp_s)/(rho g), the band's cubic root and API 520's full-lift equation) at Q = 0, at Q_f and at 201 evenly spaced flows from 0 to the flow at which the valve alone would take the spread of the fixed heads (network_check.relief_gpv_points), written by WNTR in gpm and ft with six decimals (checked against network_check.gpv_table); the connection on its inlet port is an EPANET CV pipe, which closes the path below set and on reverse flow (cvstatus). Expected relief results from EPANET's flow and heads (derivations_relief.relief_result).",
